window.FIELD_CHAPTER_PARTS["book-2-full-book"][25] = "<h1>JOURNEY 1: SAARTHI — AI Study Companion for JEE/NEET Aspirants</h1>\n<h2>23.1.1 Origin and context</h2>\n<p>The idea for Saarthi emerged from a specific observation. India has approximately 1.5 million students taking JEE Main each year, another 2 million taking NEET. The vast majority pay ₹50,000-300,000 per year for coaching at offline institutes (Allen, Aakash, FIITJEE, Resonance, etc.). The marginal student in a Tier-2 or Tier-3 city often:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Cannot access top-tier offline coaching.</li>\n<li>Studies from textbooks and YouTube videos with no personalized guidance.</li>\n<li>Has questions they can't ask anyone (parents are not domain experts; school teachers are overburdened).</li>\n<li>Practices on standardized test papers without diagnostic feedback.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Existing online platforms (Unacademy, Vedantu, PhysicsWallah, Byju's) provide content but not personalized coaching at scale. Their model is: produce great videos, sell access. The personal tutor experience is not their product.</p>\n<p>The hypothesis: an AI tutor that knows the JEE/NEET curriculum deeply, adapts to each student's weaknesses, and provides Socratic dialogue could substitute for a fraction of the human tutor experience at 10x lower cost. If even directionally correct, the addressable market is large and growing.</p>\n<p>The opening question: is this a real product, or just an obvious idea that's been tried 50 times?</p>\n<h2>23.1.2 Discovery — 40 student interviews</h2>\n<p>The first action was discovery, not building. Forty interviews over six weeks, structured as:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>25 students currently preparing for JEE or NEET.</li>\n<li>8 parents of students.</li>\n<li>5 teachers at coaching institutes.</li>\n<li>2 founders of competitor products who'd be willing to talk.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>The interview structure (60 minutes each, Mom-Test-compliant):</p>\n<p><strong>Opening 15 minutes — context.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>&quot;Walk me through your typical study week.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;What does your average day look like Monday through Sunday?&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;How do you decide what to study?&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Middle 30 minutes — pain.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>&quot;Tell me about the last time you got stuck on a problem you couldn't solve.&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;What did you do?&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;How did that feel?&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;Tell me about your last mock test. How did it go? What was the worst part?&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;What's the most frustrating thing about your prep right now?&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Last 15 minutes — workarounds and current solutions.</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>&quot;What tools or apps do you currently use?&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;What do they do well? What do they do badly?&quot;</li>\n<li>&quot;If you could wave a wand and have any kind of help, what would it look like?&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Notes: never describe Saarthi or the proposed product. Listen for jobs, pains, and current solutions. Note specific phrases students used (these become product copy later).</p>\n<h3>What the interviews revealed</h3>\n<p><strong>Real pain points (recurring across 30+ of 40 conversations):</strong></p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Lonely problem-solving.</strong> &quot;I get stuck for 30 minutes on one problem, then give up. I have nobody to ask. The coaching teacher is busy. My parents don't know physics.&quot; (Mentioned by 31 of 40.)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>No diagnostic feedback after mock tests.</strong> &quot;I get my score, but I don't know <em>why</em> I got those questions wrong. The institute doesn't go through them in detail.&quot; (28 of 40.)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Topic prioritization confusion.</strong> &quot;There's so much syllabus. I don't know what to focus on. I just follow whatever order the institute uses.&quot; (24 of 40.)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Concept gaps that compound.</strong> &quot;I realize 6 months later I never understood [Topic X], and now [Topic Y] is impossible.&quot; (22 of 40.)</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p><strong>Anxiety and motivation.</strong> &quot;I have no one to talk to about how scared I am. My parents only ask about my marks. I feel alone.&quot; (19 of 40.)</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Things students said they wanted that they probably don't really want:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>&quot;Better video content&quot; (they have unlimited content; the issue is <em>engagement</em> with content, not access).</li>\n<li>&quot;More problems to solve&quot; (they have unlimited problems; the issue is feedback on solutions).</li>\n<li>&quot;Mock tests&quot; (every institute provides these; the issue is post-test analysis).</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This is the Mom Test in action. What students <em>say</em> they want vs. what they <em>actually need</em> differ. Discovery surfaces the latter.</p>\n<p><strong>Real surprises:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>A meaningful fraction of students described the social isolation explicitly. The product was initially conceived as a study tool; it became clear the product is also a companionship tool.</li>\n<li>Several students mentioned wanting an AI that could &quot;judge them less than my parents do.&quot; Trust and non-judgmental dialogue surfaced as a feature requirement.</li>\n<li>WhatsApp as the primary expected delivery mechanism was almost universal. &quot;Why would I download a new app? I live in WhatsApp.&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Synthesis: the JTBD</h3>\n<p>After synthesizing 40 interviews, the JTBD became clear:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><em>&quot;When I'm stuck on a problem during my self-study or after a mock test, I want immediate, expert, patient guidance that helps me understand my weakness and shows me a path forward — without making me feel stupid or alone — so I can keep studying productively and trust I'm progressing.&quot;</em></p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>This is the job. Notice what's missing: there's nothing about content. Students don't lack content. They lack a tutor.</p>\n<p>The product is fundamentally not &quot;more content.&quot; It's a tutor.</p>\n<h2>23.1.3 Concept testing — 12 students with mockups</h2>\n<p>Before building anything, I built clickable Figma mockups of the core flow:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>Student opens WhatsApp, messages the bot.</li>\n<li>Bot asks: &quot;What are you stuck on?&quot; Student types or photographs the problem.</li>\n<li>Bot responds with a Socratic dialogue: not the answer, but the next step in reasoning.</li>\n<li>Student responds; bot adapts.</li>\n<li>After resolving, bot logs the interaction; over time, a profile forms of the student's strengths and weaknesses.</li>\n<li>Periodic check-ins: &quot;I notice you've struggled with rotational mechanics three times this week. Want to walk through it from scratch?&quot;</li>\n<li>Mock-test review mode: paste your answers; bot reviews each, identifies pattern.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Showed mockups to 12 of the original 40 students. Asked: &quot;If this existed today, would you use it?&quot;</p>\n<p>Reactions:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>10 of 12 said yes, with varying enthusiasm.</li>\n<li>2 said &quot;maybe&quot; — both said they'd want to see it actually work, not just mockups.</li>\n<li>One specific concern: &quot;Will it actually be helpful or will it just give me wrong answers?&quot;</li>\n</ul>\n<p>This last concern became the central product risk: AI hallucinations on technical content. If Saarthi confidently teaches a wrong concept, it's worse than nothing. The eval discipline (covered later) is non-negotiable.</p>\n<h2>23.1.4 The PRD</h2>\n<p>Below is the PRD as it would be written before building the MVP. Real-feeling, with all 15 sections.</p>\n<pre><code>PRODUCT REQUIREMENTS DOCUMENT\n\nPRODUCT: Saarthi — AI Study Companion for JEE/NEET Aspirants\nVERSION: v0.1 (MVP)\nAUTHOR: [PM name]\nDATE: April 2026\nSTATUS: Draft for review\nREVIEWERS: Eng lead, AI lead, Designer, Founder\n\n==============================================================\n\n1. ONE-LINER\n\nSaarthi is a WhatsApp-based AI tutor for Indian JEE/NEET aspirants that \nhelps students work through problems they're stuck on, identifies their \nweak topics over time, and provides personalized study guidance — at \n₹500/month vs. ₹5,000+/month for offline coaching.\n\n==============================================================\n\n2. CONTEXT\n\nThe Indian JEE/NEET prep market has ~3.5M annual aspirants and ~₹50K-3L \nARPU at offline coaching institutes. The Tier-2/3 segment (estimated \n60%+ of aspirants) is underserved: limited access to top coaching, \nparents who can't help with technical content, and anxiety from \nisolation in study.\n\nOnline incumbents (Unacademy, Vedantu, PhysicsWallah, Byju's) compete on \ncontent production. None offer personal tutoring at scale. Frontier LLMs \n(GPT-4 class, Claude Opus) can now reason through JEE/NEET-level \nproblems with sufficient grounding, opening the technical possibility of \nAI-personalized tutoring.\n\nDiscovery (40 student interviews) confirmed:\n- The pain is acute (~78% of interviews mentioned getting stuck without \n  help as a top frustration).\n- The current workarounds (YouTube, asking peers, abandoning the problem) \n  are unsatisfying.\n- Trust is high if the product proves accurate; trust collapses \n  immediately on factual errors.\n\nThe window: this market segment is digitally native, comfortable with \nWhatsApp, and price-sensitive — perfect for a low-cost AI-first product.\n\n==============================================================\n\n3. PROBLEM STATEMENT\n\nIndian JEE/NEET aspirants in Tier-2/3 cities, ages 16-19, lack access to \npersonal tutoring during their 1-2 years of preparation. They face:\n\n- 31 of 40 interviewees cited &quot;getting stuck on problems with no one to \n  ask&quot; as a top pain.\n- 28 of 40 cited absence of post-mock-test diagnostic feedback.\n- 24 of 40 cited topic prioritization confusion.\n- 22 of 40 cited cumulative concept gaps that compound over months.\n- 19 of 40 mentioned anxiety/isolation related to study.\n\nThe cost: students underperform their potential, exit JEE/NEET prep \nwithout satisfactory results despite parental investment, and develop \nnegative associations with rigorous study.\n\n==============================================================\n\n4. USERS AND USE CASES\n\nPrimary persona: &quot;Tier-2 Aspirant&quot;\n- Ages 16-19, mostly male (60/40 male/female reflecting JEE/NEET \n  participation), Tier-2/3 cities (e.g., Patna, Indore, Coimbatore, \n  Lucknow).\n- Student in Class 11 or 12, or Drop Year (gap year focused on \n  preparation).\n- Family income: ₹4-15 lakh per year (middle class).\n- Smartphone: budget Android (Xiaomi, Realme, Vivo); 4G mostly; data \n  cost-conscious.\n- Already pays ₹50,000-150,000/year for some form of coaching \n  (institute or online platform).\n- Uses WhatsApp 4+ hours/day. YouTube. Some Telegram for study groups.\n- English-comfortable but not native; Hindi/regional often preferred \n  for casual conversation.\n\nSecondary persona: &quot;Parent of Aspirant&quot;\n- Often the buyer (pays for the product); not the user.\n- Wants visibility into child's progress without micromanaging.\n- Skeptical of AI; needs reassurance about accuracy.\n\nTop 5 use cases (priority order):\n\nUC1: Stuck-on-problem help.\nStudent is solving a problem from a textbook or mock test. Stuck for \n&gt;10 minutes. Opens Saarthi on WhatsApp. Photo or types the problem. \nSaarthi guides them through the reasoning step-by-step.\n\nUC2: Mock-test review.\nStudent finishes a mock test. Pastes/photos their answers and the \nquestion paper. Saarthi identifies which questions are wrong, why, \nand groups by weak topics.\n\nUC3: Topic learning.\nStudent wants to learn or revise a topic. &quot;Explain rotational dynamics \nto me.&quot; Saarthi provides explanation, examples, and practice problems \nadapted to the student's level.\n\nUC4: Daily study planning.\n&quot;What should I study today?&quot; Saarthi reviews recent struggles, \nrecommends 60-90 minutes of focused work targeting weak areas.\n\nUC5: Motivational/anxiety check-ins.\nSaarthi periodically (1-2x per week) checks in: &quot;How are you feeling \nabout your prep?&quot; Provides empathetic listening and grounded advice.\n\n==============================================================\n\n5. PROPOSED SOLUTION\n\nSaarthi is delivered via WhatsApp. Three primary modes:\n\nA. Reactive mode (student-initiated): Student messages Saarthi with a \nproblem, photo, or question. Saarthi responds with Socratic guidance.\n\nB. Proactive mode (Saarthi-initiated): Daily check-ins, weekly progress \nsummaries, periodic anxiety check-ins.\n\nC. Review mode (mock-test): Student pastes their attempt; Saarthi \ndiagnoses each question.\n\nArchitecture:\n\n- WhatsApp Business API (via Twilio or AiSensy) for message I/O.\n- Backend: Python service with FastAPI.\n- LLM layer: GPT-4o or Claude Sonnet for reasoning; smaller model \n  (GPT-4o-mini or Claude Haiku) for routing and simple queries.\n- RAG layer: indexed corpus of NCERT textbooks, JEE/NEET past papers, \n  and curated explanations from public domain sources.\n- Eval system: 500-question gold dataset; LLM-judge pipeline; weekly \n  regression testing.\n- User profile DB: Postgres tracking topic-level mastery scores per \n  student.\n\nDefault flow for &quot;stuck on a problem&quot;:\n\nStudent: [photo of physics problem]\nSaarthi: I see you're working on a problem about [topic detected from \nOCR + analysis]. Before I help, let me check — what have you tried so \nfar? What confused you?\n\nStudent: I drew a free-body diagram but I don't know what to do next.\nSaarthi: Good — a free-body diagram is the right start. Let's look at \nit together. [If photo of diagram, OCRs it.] Can you list the forces \nyou've drawn? I want to make sure we have all of them.\n\nStudent: Gravity, normal force, friction.\nSaarthi: Great. Are there any other forces I should consider? Hint: \nthis object is on an inclined surface and being pushed.\n\n[continues until student arrives at solution OR Saarthi escalates with \nmore direct help]\n\nNote: Saarthi does NOT give the answer immediately. Socratic dialogue \nis the design choice. Direct-answer mode is available but secondary.\n\n==============================================================\n\n6. SUCCESS METRICS\n\nPrimary metric: Weekly Active Users (WAU). Target: 1,000 WAU by month \n3 of MVP launch.\n\nSecondary metrics (guardrails):\n- Resolution rate (% of student questions ending with student affirming \n  understanding): target 70%+.\n- Retention (Week 4 retention from sign-up): target 50%+.\n- Hallucination rate (manual sample review): target &lt;2% of responses.\n- Conversion (free trial → paid subscriber): target 15%+ at month 6.\n\nNegative metrics to watch:\n- Per-user inference cost: target &lt;₹400/month per active user.\n- User-reported errors (in-app &quot;report&quot; button): keep below 5% of \n  sessions.\n- Parent satisfaction: NPS &gt;40.\n\nTime horizon: monthly checkpoints; first major review at 90 days post-\nlaunch.\n\n==============================================================\n\n7. NON-GOALS\n\nSaarthi is NOT:\n\n- A content library. We will not produce video content. (Many \n  competitors do. We don't.)\n- A test-taking platform. We will not host mock tests. We will help \n  students review tests they took elsewhere.\n- An offline app. Internet-required. (We may add limited offline \n  capability later, but not in MVP.)\n- A gamified study tracker. No streaks, no leaderboards, no badges. \n  (Gamification is engagement-bait that doesn't serve actual learning.)\n- A school replacement. Saarthi supplements coaching/study; does not \n  replace.\n- For students below Class 9 or above first-year college. Out of scope.\n- A general-purpose AI chatbot. Saarthi only discusses JEE/NEET prep; \n  off-topic queries are politely deflected.\n\n==============================================================\n\n8. EDGE CASES\n\nE1: Student photo is unreadable. Behavior: Saarthi asks student to \nretake or type the question. Provides a sample of how to type math \nnotation.\n\nE2: Student question is in Hindi or regional language. Behavior: \nSaarthi accepts; responds in same language. (Initial scope: Hindi + \nEnglish. Tamil, Telugu, Bengali in v1.1.)\n\nE3: Student asks an off-topic question (e.g., &quot;What's a good movie?&quot;). \nBehavior: Saarthi politely deflects. &quot;I'm here to help with your \nJEE/NEET prep. Want to work on a topic?&quot;\n\nE4: Student asks something safety-relevant (mental health, suicidal \nideation). Behavior: Saarthi expresses care, provides Vandrevala or \niCall hotline numbers, encourages talking to trusted adult. No \nattempt to handle as therapy.\n\nE5: Student is wrong but confident. Behavior: Saarthi gently corrects \nwith explanation and verification. &quot;Let me show you why — try this \nspecific case.&quot;\n\nE6: Student tries to get Saarthi to do their homework wholesale. \nBehavior: Saarthi explains its role: &quot;I help you learn, not do your \nwork. Let's take it step by step.&quot;\n\nE7: Saarthi doesn't know the answer with confidence. Behavior: Saarthi \nexplicitly says &quot;I'm not 100% sure on this — let me try a different \napproach&quot; or &quot;Please verify this with your teacher.&quot; Does not pretend \nto know.\n\nE8: Student in poor connectivity. WhatsApp messages may delay. \nBehavior: messages queue and deliver when connection resumes; Saarthi \nis patient with delays.\n\nE9: Adversarial use — student tries to get Saarthi to misbehave \n(jailbreak prompts). Behavior: system prompt is hardened; refuses \nclearly and re-anchors to JEE/NEET prep.\n\nE10: Multi-student account sharing. Behavior: detected via usage \npattern; product requires student-level signup; family plans available \nfor siblings.\n\n==============================================================\n\n9. RISKS AND MITIGATIONS\n\nR1: Hallucinations on technical content (HIGH likelihood, HIGH impact).\nMitigation: Eval system with 500-question gold dataset; monthly \nregression testing; in-product &quot;report error&quot; button; manual sampling \nof 1% of conversations weekly; LLM-as-judge pipeline.\n\nR2: Insufficient retention (MEDIUM likelihood, HIGH impact). \nMitigation: Track Week 1, Week 4, Week 12 retention separately. If \nWeek 4 &lt; 30%, treat as kill criterion. Iterate on engagement loops.\n\nR3: Cost per user &gt; ₹400/month at scale (MEDIUM likelihood, HIGH \nimpact). Mitigation: Model cascade (cheaper model for simple queries, \nexpensive only for hard reasoning); aggressive caching of similar \nexplanations; Indian Open-weight models (e.g., Sarvam) for routing \nwhere quality permits.\n\nR4: Negative parent reaction to AI (MEDIUM likelihood, MEDIUM impact). \nMitigation: Parent-facing comms emphasizing safety and accuracy; \nweekly progress reports; explicit acknowledgment of AI limitations; \nhuman support channel.\n\nR5: Regulatory uncertainty (LOW likelihood, MEDIUM impact). The \nedtech space in India has DPDP Act implications and potential CBSE/MOE \noversight. Mitigation: Privacy by Design from day one; legal review \nof comms; no claims of &quot;guaranteed results.&quot;\n\nR6: Competitor response (Byju's, Unacademy launch competing AI). \n(MEDIUM likelihood, MEDIUM impact). Mitigation: speed and depth of \nfocus on JEE/NEET (vs broad K-12); brand and community.\n\n==============================================================\n\n10. DEPENDENCIES\n\nD1: WhatsApp Business API access (via partner like AiSensy). Lead time: \n2-3 weeks for Meta approval. Owner: Engineering.\n\nD2: NCERT textbook content (publicly available; needs cleanup and \nindexing). Lead time: 4 weeks for full corpus. Owner: AI team.\n\nD3: JEE/NEET past paper corpus (public; needs OCR and structured \nparsing). Lead time: 6 weeks. Owner: AI team.\n\nD4: Eval gold dataset (500 questions with expert-validated solutions). \nLead time: 4 weeks. Need to hire 2 expert reviewers (subject matter \nexperts in physics, chemistry, biology, math). Owner: PM + AI team.\n\nD5: Payment integration (Razorpay for ₹500/month subscription). Lead \ntime: 1 week. Owner: Engineering.\n\n==============================================================\n\n11. ROLLOUT PLAN\n\nPhase 1 (Weeks 1-4 post-build): Closed alpha with 30 students recruited \nfrom original interview pool. Free access. Heavy feedback collection.\n\nPhase 2 (Weeks 5-8): Open beta with 200 students. Free 30-day trial. \nMonitor metrics; iterate.\n\nPhase 3 (Weeks 9-12): Public launch. ₹500/month subscription with \n14-day free trial. Marketing through Instagram + JEE/NEET YouTube \ncreators + WhatsApp study groups.\n\nPhase 4 (Months 4-6): Geographic expansion (start in Bihar/UP/Tamil \nNadu Tier-2 cities; expand based on demand).\n\n==============================================================\n\n12. EXPERIMENTS / VALIDATION\n\nPre-launch:\n- Concierge MVP test (Weeks 1-2): I personally respond to 5 students' \n  questions over WhatsApp, handcrafted, mimicking what Saarthi would \n  do. Validate the format works.\n- LLM benchmark (Week 1-2): Test GPT-4o, Claude Sonnet, and \n  open-weight Sarvam on 100 sample JEE/NEET problems. Pick the model \n  with best correctness × cost ratio.\n\nPost-launch (in beta):\n- Socratic vs direct-answer A/B test: half of beta users get default \n  Socratic; half get default direct-answer. Measure satisfaction, \n  retention, learning outcome (self-reported and via mock test \n  performance).\n- Pricing test (Phase 3): some users see ₹300, some see ₹500, some \n  see ₹700/month. Measure conversion.\n\n==============================================================\n\n13. OPEN QUESTIONS\n\nQ1: Should we offer a parent-facing dashboard? Pro: parental visibility \nmight increase willingness to pay. Con: students may feel surveilled, \nreducing trust. Decision pending after Phase 1.\n\nQ2: Should free tier exist? Currently scoping 14-day free trial only. \nConsider freemium (limited messages/day) if conversion is too low.\n\nQ3: How do we handle academically dishonest use (Saarthi solving \nhomework wholesale)? Initial behavior is to refuse; may need to revisit \nbased on student behavior data.\n\nQ4: What's our long-term position vs. content platforms (Unacademy, \nPhysicsWallah)? Are we their replacement or complement?\n\n==============================================================\n\n14. APPENDICES\n\nA. Discovery interview notes (40 interviews, anonymized).\nB. JTBD synthesis.\nC. Concept test results (12 students).\nD. Competitor analysis (10 products).\nE. AI architecture detailed design.\nF. Eval framework design.\nG. Privacy review checklist.\n\n==============================================================\n\n15. SIGN-OFFS\n\n[ ] Engineering lead\n[ ] AI lead\n[ ] Design lead\n[ ] Founder\n[ ] Legal/compliance\n\n</code></pre>\n<p>This PRD represents about 8-10 hours of dedicated work. Most PRDs at most companies are weaker. This one is real-feeling: it has specifics, it makes commitments, it acknowledges uncertainty, it explicitly lists what we won't do.</p>\n<h2>23.1.5 AI architecture decisions</h2>\n<p>The AI architecture is where the product lives or dies. Here are the key decisions, with explicit trade-offs.</p>\n<h3>Decision 1: Frontier model vs open-weight model</h3>\n<p><strong>Choice:</strong> Frontier model (GPT-4o or Claude Sonnet) for primary reasoning.</p>\n<p><strong>Reasoning:</strong> JEE/NEET physics, chemistry, biology, and math problems require strong reasoning. Open-weight models (Llama 3.1, Mistral, Sarvam) at the time of writing are still ~10-20% behind frontier models on technical reasoning. The cost difference (₹50/M tokens vs ₹500/M tokens) is significant but the quality gap matters more for our use case — wrong physics is worse than expensive physics.</p>\n<p><strong>Trade-off accepted:</strong> Higher per-query cost (~₹2-4 per student question vs ~₹0.30 for open-weight). This pushes our unit economics: at ₹500/month subscription and ~30 questions/student/day, COGS is ~₹120/month/student. Margin is workable but not lavish.</p>\n<p><strong>Mitigation:</strong> Cascade architecture. Use a smaller/cheaper model for routing decisions (which subject, what type of question, easy/hard). Only invoke the frontier model for the actual reasoning. This cuts cost ~40%.</p>\n<h3>Decision 2: RAG vs fine-tuning vs in-context</h3>\n<p><strong>Choice:</strong> RAG primary; in-context for few-shot examples; no fine-tuning in v1.</p>\n<p><strong>Reasoning:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>The JEE/NEET corpus (NCERT textbooks, past papers, key reference materials) is large and specific. RAG is the right tool to ground responses.</li>\n<li>Fine-tuning would require curated training data we don't have at v1 scale. Also: fine-tuning a frontier model is expensive and we lose model upgrades automatically.</li>\n<li>Few-shot examples in the prompt help with format and tone.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Trade-off accepted:</strong> RAG quality depends entirely on retrieval quality. Bad retrieval = bad response. We invest heavily in:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Chunk strategy (per-section for textbooks; per-question for past papers).</li>\n<li>Hybrid search (semantic + keyword) because exact terms matter (&quot;rotational kinematics&quot; should retrieve rotation-specific content).</li>\n<li>Re-ranking (use a cross-encoder to re-rank top-K results).</li>\n</ul>\n<h3>Decision 3: Eval framework</h3>\n<p><strong>Choice:</strong> 500-question gold dataset, expanded over time; LLM-as-judge for routine eval; human review for 1% sample weekly; regression testing on every prompt change.</p>\n<p><strong>Why this matters more than the model choice:</strong> The model is interchangeable; the eval set is what tells us if we improved. We invest 4 weeks upfront to build the gold dataset (paying expert tutors to validate), and continue expanding.</p>\n<p><strong>The eval categories:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Accuracy: Is the final answer correct?</li>\n<li>Reasoning quality: Are the steps sound?</li>\n<li>Pedagogical quality: Does it teach without giving answers prematurely?</li>\n<li>Tone: Is it patient, non-judgmental?</li>\n<li>Safety: Does it refuse to handle adversarial prompts cleanly?</li>\n</ul>\n<p>For each category, score 1-5 by LLM-judge (calibrated against human review samples).</p>\n<h3>Decision 4: Hallucination defense layers</h3>\n<p>The most important problem in this product. Layered defenses:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Grounding via RAG.</strong> Most factual content comes from indexed corpus; reduces hallucination.</li>\n<li><strong>Citation.</strong> When Saarthi references a textbook fact, it cites: &quot;From NCERT Class 12 Physics, Chapter 5...&quot;</li>\n<li><strong>Confidence prompting.</strong> System prompt explicitly tells the model to say &quot;I'm not sure&quot; when uncertain.</li>\n<li><strong>Verification step.</strong> For numerical answers, a second model pass verifies the calculation.</li>\n<li><strong>User reporting.</strong> In-app button to flag errors. Each flag triggers human review and (if confirmed) addition to a &quot;regression test&quot; suite.</li>\n<li><strong>Manual sampling.</strong> 1% of conversations are reviewed weekly by a human expert.</li>\n<li><strong>Topic boundaries.</strong> Saarthi explicitly does not engage with questions outside the JEE/NEET syllabus.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>These are layers because no single defense is sufficient. Defense in depth.</p>\n<h3>Decision 5: Latency target</h3>\n<p><strong>Choice:</strong> Median response time &lt;8 seconds; P95 &lt;15 seconds.</p>\n<p><strong>Why these numbers:</strong> For a chat experience, anything over 10 seconds feels broken. WhatsApp's expected cadence is fast. But: deep reasoning can't be rushed without quality cost. Streaming partial responses (showing &quot;thinking...&quot; then incremental output) helps perceived latency.</p>\n<p><strong>Trade-off accepted:</strong> Some queries are slow because reasoning is slow. We optimize what we can (model choice, prompt length, parallel retrieval) and accept the rest.</p>\n<h2>23.1.6 The metrics tree</h2>\n<pre><code>                    Active monthly subscribers\n                              |\n          ___________________|____________________\n         |                                        |\n    New paying users                       Retained subscribers\n         |                                        |\n      ___|___                              _______|_______\n     |       |                            |               |\n   Free   Trial→                       Daily         Renewal\n   trial  paid                         engagement    rate\n   sign-  conversion                  (sessions/day)\n   ups   rate                              |\n     |                                _____|_____\n     |                               |           |\n   Acquisition                  Resolution    Per-session\n   channel                      rate (% of    duration/depth\n   performance                  questions\n                                resolved)\n</code></pre>\n<p>The North-Star Metric: <strong>Active monthly subscribers</strong> (proxy for value delivered + business sustainability).</p>\n<p>The leading indicator we watch most carefully: <strong>Resolution rate × Per-session duration</strong>. If users are getting their problems resolved and engaging deeply, retention and conversion follow.</p>\n<p>The early warning indicator: <strong>Hallucination rate × User error reports</strong>. If these climb, retention is about to crater regardless of what other metrics show.</p>\n<h2>23.1.7 The MVP plan with kill criteria</h2>\n<p><strong>MVP Scope (4 months to launch):</strong></p>\n<p>Month 1: Core architecture</p>\n<ul>\n<li>WhatsApp integration.</li>\n<li>LLM cascade.</li>\n<li>Basic RAG over NCERT.</li>\n<li>Initial system prompts.</li>\n<li>100-question eval set.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Month 2: Core flow</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stuck-on-problem flow.</li>\n<li>OCR for problem photos.</li>\n<li>User profiling and topic mastery tracking.</li>\n<li>Concierge MVP with 5 students.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Month 3: Polish + closed alpha</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Mock-test review flow.</li>\n<li>Daily check-ins.</li>\n<li>Eval set expanded to 500.</li>\n<li>30 students in closed alpha.</li>\n<li>Internal hardening based on alpha feedback.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Month 4: Open beta + payment</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Public launch in 1-2 cities.</li>\n<li>Razorpay integration.</li>\n<li>Pricing test.</li>\n<li>200 users target by end of month.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>Kill Criteria (decide at month 3 of public launch, ~month 7 from start):</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>If Week 4 retention &lt;30%: kill. The product isn't fundamentally landing.</li>\n<li>If hallucination rate &gt;5%: kill or radical re-architecture.</li>\n<li>If per-user cost &gt; ₹600/month against ₹500 ARPU: kill or reprice.</li>\n<li>If conversion (trial to paid) &lt;8%: kill or fundamentally rethink value prop.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>If any of those triggers, we stop, learn, and either pivot or shut down. Pivots considered: B2B (selling to coaching institutes); narrower scope (just mock-test review); broader scope (general K-12 tutor).</p>\n<h2>23.1.8 Trade-offs we accepted</h2>\n<p>Eight specific trade-offs, named explicitly:</p>\n<ol>\n<li><strong>Quality &gt; Speed.</strong> Used frontier model despite cost. Will hurt margin until scale.</li>\n<li><strong>Socratic &gt; Direct.</strong> Default to Socratic dialogue, even though students sometimes prefer the direct answer. We're betting on learning outcomes over instant gratification.</li>\n<li><strong>WhatsApp &gt; App.</strong> Lower friction on adoption; less control over UX; stuck with WhatsApp's constraints.</li>\n<li><strong>JEE/NEET only &gt; broader.</strong> Focused scope; missing addressable market in K-12 broadly. Bet that depth in JEE/NEET wins.</li>\n<li><strong>Subscription &gt; freemium.</strong> Risk lower acquisition; better unit economics. Will revisit if conversion is low.</li>\n<li><strong>Eval-first &gt; ship-fast.</strong> 4 weeks on gold dataset before MVP. Slower start; better quality.</li>\n<li><strong>No video content.</strong> Limits what we can offer relative to competitors. Bet that tutoring is differentiated enough to win.</li>\n<li><strong>No gamification.</strong> Possibly lower engagement; bet that real learning &gt; engagement-bait.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Each is a real trade-off. Each has costs we accept.</p>\n<h2>23.1.9 Post-launch retrospective (hypothetical, real-feeling)</h2>\n<p>After 9 months in market:</p>\n<p><strong>What worked:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Stuck-on-problem flow was the killer feature. 78% of student WAU touched this flow weekly.</li>\n<li>Mock-test review was second strongest. Higher session depth than expected.</li>\n<li>WhatsApp distribution was correct. Friction-free adoption.</li>\n<li>Hallucination rate stayed under 1.5% — better than target. Eval discipline paid off.</li>\n<li>Word-of-mouth in study groups was a real acquisition channel.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What didn't:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Daily check-ins were almost universally ignored. Removed in v1.2.</li>\n<li>Anxiety/motivation flow was used by maybe 5% of users; dropped as a default.</li>\n<li>Parent dashboard launched in v1.1; very low usage. Repackaged as a weekly digest email instead.</li>\n<li>Conversion (trial → paid) settled at 11% — below our 15% target. Forced us to extend trial to 30 days and repackage entry-tier.</li>\n<li>Retention W4 was 38%, below 50% target. Investigation showed: students who used Saarthi 3+ times in first week retained at 60%+; students who used it 1-2 times retained at 15%. Conclusion: onboarding push needed to drive deeper first-week engagement.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What we changed:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Onboarding now includes a guided first-problem walkthrough (pre-set sample problem walked through Socratically). First-week engagement up 30%.</li>\n<li>Pricing repackaged: ₹400/month basic (with monthly question cap), ₹800/month unlimited. Conversion improved.</li>\n<li>Removed daily check-ins; added &quot;weekly progress&quot; digest instead. Less intrusive, similar visibility for parents.</li>\n<li>Geographic expansion: started Hindi-language UI in v1.1. Added Tamil and Telugu in v1.3.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What we'd do differently from scratch:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Move to onboarding-driven engagement push earlier. We assumed students would use the product naturally; many didn't.</li>\n<li>Less ambitious feature scope at MVP. Daily check-ins and motivation flows distracted from the core &quot;tutor&quot; use case.</li>\n<li>Earlier WhatsApp Group integration. Many students study in groups; we eventually built a &quot;study group&quot; mode in v1.4 that drove retention.</li>\n<li>More aggressive eval expansion. We launched with 500 questions; should have launched with 1500.</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What's next:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Voice input/output for students who type slowly.</li>\n<li>Parent-facing companion (separate WhatsApp number) with summaries and concerns.</li>\n<li>Tier-3 city expansion with vernacular-first onboarding.</li>\n<li>Long-term: school/coaching partnerships for B2B distribution.</li>\n</ul>\n<h2>23.1.10 What would Google look for here?</h2>\n<p>If you were a Google APM candidate presenting Saarthi as your product story, what signals does this case study send?</p>\n<p><strong>Strong signals:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>Discovery rigor (40 interviews, JTBD synthesis, concept testing before building).</li>\n<li>AI fluency (clear cost/quality/latency thinking, eval-driven development, layered hallucination defense).</li>\n<li>Trade-off explicitness (8 named trade-offs with costs accepted).</li>\n<li>Metric thinking (NSM tree, leading indicators, kill criteria).</li>\n<li>Indian-context awareness (WhatsApp distribution, Tier-2/3 focus, vernacular).</li>\n<li>Honest retrospective (named what didn't work, not just what did).</li>\n</ul>\n<p><strong>What would prompt follow-up questions:</strong></p>\n<ul>\n<li>How did you decide on the ₹500 price point specifically? (Test of strategic pricing reasoning.)</li>\n<li>What if a competitor with deeper pockets (e.g., Byju's) launches a similar AI product 6 months later? (Test of competitive strategic thinking.)</li>\n<li>The retention number is below target. Beyond what you mentioned, what other hypotheses could explain it? (Test of analytical rigor.)</li>\n<li>Why Socratic over direct? Walk me through the trade-off more deeply. (Test of educational philosophy + product taste.)</li>\n<li>What's your view on the long-term defensibility of an AI-tutor product? (Test of strategic thinking on AI moats.)</li>\n</ul>\n<p>A strong candidate handles these follow-ups with specific reasoning, not generic answers. The Saarthi case study, fully internalized, prepares you for all of these.</p>\n<p>This is what an end-to-end product journey looks like when documented. Notice what's not here: theatrics, big numbers, magical insights. Just discovery → decisions → outcomes → learning.</p>\n<p>The next four journeys follow the same pattern in different domains. Each should expand your range. Read all five.</p>\n<hr>\n<p><em>End of Journey 1. Journey 2 — ClearOps — begins next.</em></p>\n<p>\\newpage</p>\n";
